-- Q4690: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The information is complete and accurate in every material detail","The information is fairly stated in relation to the financial statements as a whole","The information complies with all applicable regulatory requirements","The information is more reliable than the basic financial statements themselves"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 725, when reporting on supplementary information in relation to the audited financial statements as a whole, the auditor opines on whether the information is fairly stated, in all material respects, in relation to the financial statements as a whole. This is a lower threshold than an opinion on the supplementary information taken by itself.
Wrong (A): The opinion does not address completeness and accuracy in all respects; the standard uses the ''fairly stated in relation to'' framework.
Wrong (C): The opinion relates to the financial statements as a whole, not to regulatory compliance.
Wrong (D): The auditor does not opine that supplementary information is more reliable than the basic statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4690;

-- Q4692: Other Information and Supplementary Information [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Note the omission in the auditor''s report without modifying the opinion on financial statements","Issue a qualified opinion on the financial statements due to the omission","Refuse to issue the audit report until the RSI is included","Issue an adverse opinion on the financial statements as a whole"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 730, if required supplementary information is omitted, the auditor should include an other-matter paragraph in the report noting the omission. The omission does not modify the opinion on the basic financial statements because RSI is not part of the basic financial statements.
Wrong (B): Omission of RSI does not warrant a qualified opinion since RSI is supplementary, not part of the basic financial statements.
Wrong (C): The auditor is not required to refuse to issue the report; the appropriate response is to note the omission.
Wrong (D): An adverse opinion addresses material misstatements in the financial statements, not missing RSI.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4692;

-- Q4695: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An integral component of the audited financial statements","Required supplementary information mandated by GASB standards","Other information the auditor reads for material inconsistencies","Information entirely outside the auditor''s professional consideration"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 720, an unaudited five-year financial summary in the annual report is considered other information. The auditor should read it and consider whether it is materially inconsistent with the audited financial statements or the auditor''s knowledge obtained during the audit.
Wrong (A): The five-year summary is not part of the audited financial statements; it is unaudited supplemental content.
Wrong (B): A five-year summary is not required supplementary information under GASB; RSI is specifically prescribed by standard-setters.
Wrong (D): The auditor has a responsibility to read other information for inconsistencies and cannot ignore it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4695;

-- Q4696: Other Information and Supplementary Information [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue a qualified opinion on the financial statements themselves","Accept management''s decision without taking any further action","Describe the inconsistency in an other-matter paragraph or take other appropriate action","Adjust the financial statements to agree with the other information"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 720.A18, if management refuses to correct a material inconsistency in the other information and the financial statements are correct, the auditor should include a description of the inconsistency in an other-matter paragraph or take other actions such as withholding the report or withdrawing from the engagement.
Wrong (A): A qualified opinion addresses issues in the financial statements, not in the other information when the statements are correct.
Wrong (B): The auditor cannot simply accept a known material inconsistency without taking action.
Wrong (D): If the financial statements are correct, adjusting them to match erroneous other information would introduce a misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4696;

-- Q4697: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["May be in a separate report or an other-matter paragraph in the auditor''s report","Must always appear in a report separate from the auditor''s report","Must be combined with the financial statement opinion in a single paragraph","Cannot appear anywhere within the auditor''s report on financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 725, the auditor''s opinion on supplementary information may be presented either in a separate report or as an other-matter paragraph in the auditor''s report on the financial statements. Either format is acceptable.
Wrong (B): A separate report is one option but is not mandatory; the opinion may also appear in the auditor''s main report.
Wrong (C): The supplementary information opinion and the financial statement opinion are distinct and should not be combined into a single paragraph.
Wrong (D): Presenting the supplementary information opinion within the auditor''s report is expressly permitted by AU-C 725.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4697;

-- Q4698: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor bears no professional responsibility for other information included","The auditor must verify the accuracy of all other information presented","The auditor performs a separate audit and issues an opinion on other information","The auditor reads other information for inconsistencies without auditing or opining on it"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 720, the auditor''s responsibility for other information is limited to reading it and considering whether it is materially inconsistent with the audited financial statements or the auditor''s knowledge. The auditor does not audit or express an opinion on it.
Wrong (A): The auditor does have a responsibility for other information—specifically, to read it and consider inconsistencies.
Wrong (B): The auditor is not responsible for ensuring accuracy of all other information; the responsibility is limited to identifying material inconsistencies.
Wrong (C): The auditor does not perform a separate audit of other information or issue an opinion on it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4698;

-- Q4699: Other Information and Supplementary Information [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Part of the basic financial statements subject to the audit opinion","Other information requiring no additional reporting by the auditor","Supplementary information reported on in relation to the financial statements as a whole","Required supplementary information prescribed by GASB pronouncements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200), the schedule of expenditures of federal awards is supplementary information that the auditor reports on in relation to the financial statements as a whole. The auditor issues an opinion on whether the schedule is fairly stated in all material respects.
Wrong (A): The schedule is not part of the basic financial statements; it accompanies them as supplementary information.
Wrong (B): The schedule does require additional reporting by the auditor under the Uniform Guidance, unlike ordinary other information.
Wrong (D): The schedule is not RSI under GASB; it is supplementary information required by federal audit standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4699;

-- Q4700: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Perform audit procedures on the supplementary information regardless of engagement terms","Request that management remove the supplementary information from the document","Disregard the supplementary information entirely during the engagement","Add an other-matter paragraph stating the information was not audited and no opinion is expressed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 725, when supplementary information accompanies audited financial statements but the auditor was not engaged to report on it, the auditor should include an other-matter paragraph indicating the information was not subjected to auditing procedures and no opinion or assurance is provided.
Wrong (A): The auditor has no obligation to audit supplementary information not covered by the engagement terms.
Wrong (B): The auditor cannot require management to remove supplementary information from the document.
Wrong (C): The auditor should not ignore the information; a disclaimer paragraph is needed to avoid the impression that it was audited.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4700;

-- Q4701: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The other information contradicts the audited financial statements in a potentially material way","The other information uses different formatting than the audited financial statements","The other information provides more detail than the audited financial statements","The other information was printed or distributed separately from the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 720, a material inconsistency exists when the other information contradicts information in the audited financial statements in a way that could undermine their credibility. Revenue growth of 12% versus a 3% decline is a clear contradiction requiring auditor action.
Wrong (B): Formatting differences such as layout or font are not material inconsistencies under auditing standards.
Wrong (C): Greater detail in other information does not constitute a contradiction with the financial statements.
Wrong (D): The medium of distribution or printing has no bearing on whether a material inconsistency exists.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4701;

-- Q4702: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The RSI has been fully audited alongside the basic financial statements","The auditor guarantees the accuracy and completeness of the RSI presented","The RSI provides more reliable information than the basic financial statements","Limited procedures were applied to the RSI but it was not audited or assured"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 730, the auditor''s report on RSI should clearly state that the auditor applied certain limited procedures (inquiries of management and comparison for consistency) but did not audit the RSI and does not express an opinion or provide any assurance on it.
Wrong (A): The RSI is not audited; the auditor applies only limited procedures such as inquiries and comparisons.
Wrong (B): The auditor does not guarantee the accuracy or completeness of RSI under any circumstance.
Wrong (C): RSI is not characterized as more reliable than the basic financial statements; it is supplementary in nature.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4702;

-- Q4703: Other Information and Supplementary Information [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Has no remaining responsibility for the other information after the report date","Must re-audit the financial statements before releasing the annual report","Must reissue the auditor''s report with a revised date reflecting new information","Must still read the other information and consider it for material inconsistencies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 720, even if other information is received after the date of the auditor''s report, the auditor still has a responsibility to read it and consider whether there is a material inconsistency or misstatement of fact. If identified, the auditor should take appropriate action.
Wrong (A): The auditor''s responsibility for other information extends beyond the report date when the information is received before the financial statements are issued.
Wrong (B): Receiving other information after the report date does not require re-auditing the financial statements.
Wrong (C): The auditor is not required to reissue the report solely because other information is received later.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4703;

-- Q4704: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A matter requiring no further action by the auditor","A normal difference between narrative descriptions and quantitative data","A basis for modifying the opinion on the audited financial statements","A material inconsistency the auditor should discuss with management and resolve"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 720, a contradiction between the MD&A''s description of declining profit margins and the financial statements'' evidence of stable margins is a material inconsistency. The auditor should discuss the matter with management and request revision of the incorrect information.
Wrong (A): A material inconsistency between other information and the financial statements requires auditor action, not inaction.
Wrong (B): This is not a normal narrative-quantitative difference; it is a direct factual contradiction about financial performance trends.
Wrong (C): The audit opinion addresses the financial statements, not the other information; a material inconsistency in other information does not itself modify the opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4704;

-- Q4705: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the rate because management has the best knowledge of the property","Disclaim an opinion because fair value measurements are too subjective to audit","Require management to adopt the highest available market discount rate","Assess reasonableness by comparing to market rates and evaluating for management bias"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 540, when management uses an unusually low discount rate in a fair value measurement, the auditor should evaluate its reasonableness by comparing to market benchmarks, considering the asset''s specific risk characteristics, and assessing whether management bias may exist toward higher valuations.
Wrong (A): The auditor cannot simply defer to management''s knowledge; independent evaluation of key assumptions is required under AU-C 540.
Wrong (B): Fair value measurements are auditable; the auditor evaluates the reasonableness of assumptions rather than disclaiming.
Wrong (C): The standard does not require using the highest rate; it requires that the rate used be within a reasonable range.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4705;

-- Q5023: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether it masks an earnings trend or affects covenant or regulatory compliance","Whether it was detected through analytical procedures rather than substantive testing","Whether the misstatement originated in a prior reporting period","Whether similar misstatements exist at other companies in the same industry"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 450.A16-.A17, qualitative factors may cause a quantitatively immaterial misstatement to be considered material. These include whether the misstatement masks an earnings trend, converts a loss to income, affects regulatory compliance, or conceals management compensation.
Wrong (B): The detection method does not affect whether a misstatement is qualitatively material; the focus is on the misstatement''s nature and impact.
Wrong (C): Whether a misstatement originated in the prior period is relevant to the rollover approach but is not a qualitative materiality factor.
Wrong (D): The existence of similar misstatements at other companies is irrelevant to the entity-specific qualitative assessment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5023;

-- Q5026: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate fraud implications on other audit areas and reliability of management representations","Conclude the misstatement is immaterial because it falls below the materiality threshold","Report the fraud directly to law enforcement before communicating with management","Reclassify the misstatement as a judgmental difference involving management estimates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 240.35-.36, when the auditor identifies a misstatement resulting from fraud, the auditor must consider implications for other aspects of the audit, particularly the reliability of management representations. Intentional fraud cannot be evaluated on quantitative grounds alone because it raises questions about management integrity.
Wrong (B): A fraud-related misstatement is never evaluated solely on its dollar amount; the intentional nature elevates its significance regardless of the materiality threshold.
Wrong (C): The auditor''s primary obligation is to communicate fraud findings to appropriate levels of management and those charged with governance, not directly to law enforcement.
Wrong (D): An intentional reclassification of personal expenses is a factual misstatement due to fraud, not a judgmental difference involving estimates.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5026;

-- Q5292: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The reversal eliminates the misstatement entirely, requiring no current-year consideration","The iron curtain approach still requires evaluating the cumulative balance sheet effect","Management must restate prior-year financial statements before the current-year opinion issues","The reversal automatically converts the misstatement from factual to projected classification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 450.A16, the iron curtain approach evaluates the cumulative effect on the ending balance sheet, while the rollover approach focuses on the current-year income statement effect. Even though the income statement impact reverses, the balance sheet may still carry effects of the prior-year misstatement.
Wrong (A): A reversal in the income statement does not eliminate the need to evaluate cumulative balance sheet effects under the iron curtain methodology.
Wrong (C): Restatement is not automatically required; the auditor evaluates whether uncorrected misstatements are material in the aggregate.
Wrong (D): A reversal does not change the classification of a misstatement from factual to projected; these are distinct categories.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5292;

-- Q6139: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A material weakness regardless of any compensating controls that management has implemented","Fully mitigated by the compensating control, requiring no communication to those charged with governance","Classified based solely on the volume of transactions below the compensating review threshold","At least a significant deficiency, though the compensating control may prevent material weakness classification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 265.A10, compensating controls can mitigate the severity of a control deficiency. While lack of segregation of duties is inherently a deficiency, an effective compensating control such as detailed management review may reduce the likelihood of material misstatement. However, the underlying deficiency still warrants communication as at least a significant deficiency.
Wrong (A): AU-C 265 requires the auditor to consider the totality of controls, including compensating controls, before classifying severity; automatic material weakness classification is incorrect.
Wrong (B): Compensating controls may reduce severity but do not eliminate the deficiency or the requirement to communicate it.
Wrong (C): Classification depends on both magnitude and likelihood of misstatement, not solely on transaction volume below the review threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 6139;

-- Q6233: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A deficiency not requiring communication because cash transactions are routine in nature","A significant deficiency mitigated by the bookkeeper''s competence and trustworthiness","At least a significant deficiency, and likely a material weakness given the transaction volume","An operational matter that falls outside the scope of the financial statement audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 265.A12, a lack of segregation of duties over significant asset classes is an indicator of at least a significant deficiency. When one person controls recording, custody, and reconciliation of $6.5 million in cash—far exceeding materiality—there is a reasonable possibility of material misstatement, meeting the definition of a material weakness.
Wrong (A): The routine nature of transactions does not reduce the severity of a segregation of duties deficiency over a material asset class.
Wrong (B): Personal attributes such as competence and trustworthiness cannot mitigate a structural control weakness under auditing standards.
Wrong (D): Segregation of duties over financial assets is within the scope of the financial statement audit and is not merely an operational matter.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 6233;

-- Q6406: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disregard the reversal because the misstatement was already evaluated in the prior year","Evaluate how the reversal affects current-year income combined with any new misstatements","Accept the favorable impact because it reduces current-year risk of material misstatement","Require a prior-period adjustment to eliminate the inventory carryforward effect entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 450.10-.11, the auditor must evaluate prior-period uncorrected misstatements that affect current-period financial statements. A reversing overstatement that inflates current-year income creates a misstatement of current-period operating results, and the auditor must consider how carryforward effects interact with new misstatements.
Wrong (A): Prior-year uncorrected misstatements that reverse into the current year must be considered in the current-year evaluation, not disregarded.
Wrong (C): A favorable reversal does not reduce audit risk; it may create a current-year income overstatement that must be evaluated.
Wrong (D): A prior-period adjustment is a management decision; the auditor evaluates aggregate misstatements rather than requiring specific adjustments.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 6406;

-- Q6422: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Not reportable because no actual misstatement has resulted from the control weakness","A significant deficiency because it involves a segregation of duties conflict only","A material weakness given the substantial transaction magnitude and reasonable possibility of misstatement","Indeterminate until the auditor performs additional testing of the control environment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 265.A8, severity depends on the magnitude of the potential misstatement and the likelihood of occurrence. Although no misstatement was detected, the lack of segregation of duties over $8 million in annual transactions creates both a high magnitude of potential misstatement and a reasonable possibility of occurrence.
Wrong (A): Deficiency evaluation considers potential misstatements, not only those actually detected; a control weakness is reportable based on what could occur.
Wrong (B): Classifying this as only a significant deficiency understates the risk given the substantial dollar volume that could be affected without independent review.
Wrong (D): The auditor has sufficient information to classify the deficiency based on the transaction magnitude and the nature of the control gap.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 6422;

-- Q6465: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The selective correction pattern suggests possible management bias requiring further evaluation","The uncorrected misstatements are acceptable because they fall well below materiality","The auditor must independently correct the misstatements regardless of management''s decisions","Management''s correction of some misstatements demonstrates good faith and eliminates bias concerns"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 450.A13, the auditor considers whether the pattern of uncorrected misstatements indicates possible management bias. Selectively correcting only income-decreasing misstatements while leaving income-increasing ones uncorrected is a strong indicator of bias that could affect the overall materiality assessment.
Wrong (B): Even though the uncorrected amount is below materiality, the pattern of selective correction raises qualitative concerns that must be evaluated.
Wrong (C): The auditor cannot independently correct misstatements in the financial statements; correction is management''s responsibility.
Wrong (D): Correcting some misstatements does not negate bias concerns when the pattern of corrections consistently favors a particular financial outcome.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 6465;

-- Q7604: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Unreasonable because it falls below the midpoint of the auditor''s independently developed range","Within the reasonable range and not materially misstated, though the low-end pattern warrants attention","Required to be adjusted to the midpoint of the auditor''s range to eliminate potential bias","Disallowed entirely because the reasonable range exceeds 10% of the recorded liability amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 540, when the auditor develops a range, any point within that range is considered reasonable. Management''s $3.9 million estimate falls within the $3.8M–$5.1M range and is therefore acceptable. However, the auditor should evaluate whether a pattern of estimates consistently at the low end across multiple accounts indicates management bias.
Wrong (A): Falling below the midpoint does not make an estimate unreasonable; the entire range represents acceptable outcomes under AU-C 540.
Wrong (C): There is no requirement to adjust estimates to the midpoint; any amount within the reasonable range is acceptable.
Wrong (D): There is no 10% threshold for disallowing estimates; the standard evaluates whether the estimate falls within the auditor''s range.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7604;

-- Q7612: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No action is required because the fire occurred after the auditor''s report date","Adjust the 2025 financial statements to recognize the $6.2 million uninsured loss","Evaluate whether the fire is disclosed as a Type II subsequent event and assess going concern","Require Creekside to delay financial statement issuance until insurance claims are resolved"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 560, facts discovered after the report date but before financial statement issuance require the auditor to evaluate whether the statements need revision. A March 5 fire is a Type II subsequent event requiring note disclosure rather than adjustment. Given the 40% asset loss, the auditor should also evaluate going concern implications.
Wrong (A): The auditor has a responsibility for subsequent events through the financial statement issuance date, not just the report date.
Wrong (B): A post-year-end fire is a Type II event reflecting conditions arising after the balance sheet date; it requires disclosure, not adjustment of the financial statements.
Wrong (D): Delaying issuance until the insurance claim is resolved is not required; appropriate disclosure of the event is sufficient.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7612;

-- Q7939: Written Representations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Have been independently verified by a third-party pricing service","Are reasonable and reflect management''s intent and ability regarding specific actions","Will produce valuations consistent with observable quoted market prices","Were jointly selected by the auditor and subsequently approved by management"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 540.22 and AU-C 580.A9, the auditor should obtain management representations that the significant assumptions used in fair value measurements are reasonable and reflect management''s intent and ability to carry out specific courses of action relevant to the valuation.
Wrong (A): Independent verification by a pricing service is a substantive audit procedure, not a management representation required by the standard.
Wrong (C): Level 3 fair value measurements by definition lack quoted market prices; requiring valuations to match quoted prices is inapplicable.
Wrong (D): Assumptions are management''s responsibility; the auditor evaluates but does not select assumptions for management.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 7939;

-- Q8320: Written Representations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s verbal assurances and proceed with the current-year audit normally","Withdraw from the engagement because the prior-year deficiency signals unreliable management","Obtain a representation letter covering all periods presented, including prior-year fraud and related party items","Issue a disclaimer on prior-year comparatives and an unmodified opinion on the current year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 580.14, written representations cover all periods referred to in the auditor''s report. When comparative financial statements are presented, the successor auditor should obtain representations covering all periods for which an opinion is issued, including specific representations about fraud and related parties for the prior period.
Wrong (A): Verbal assurances are insufficient; AU-C 580 requires written representations, and the deficiency in the prior-year letter must be remediated.
Wrong (B): Omissions in the prior-year representation letter do not necessarily indicate management unreliability warranting withdrawal.
Wrong (D): Issuing a disclaimer is not required when the auditor can obtain proper representations covering the prior period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8320;

-- Q8330: Written Representations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Backdate the representation letter to align with the March 10 report date","Consider revising the report date to March 15 or obtain supplemental representations covering the gap","Restart the engagement from the planning phase because the timing invalidates the audit","Issue the report without the signed letter and document the delay in the workpapers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 580.15, the representation letter should be dated no earlier than the date of the auditor''s report. If the letter is received after the report date, the auditor should either revise the report date or obtain a letter dated as of the original report date with supplemental coverage through the receipt date.
Wrong (A): Backdating the representation letter is inappropriate because it falsifies the date on which management actually signed and provided representations.
Wrong (C): A timing discrepancy between the report and representation letter dates does not invalidate the entire engagement or require restarting.
Wrong (D): Issuing the report without the required signed representation letter violates AU-C 580; the letter is a prerequisite.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8330;

-- Q8669: Written Representations [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue a disclaimer for the full year because the former CFO refused representations","Obtain full-year representations from the current CFO and perform additional pre-October procedures","Limit the audit scope to October through December and disclose the scope restriction","Accept the former CFO''s refusal and disclose it as a subsequent event in the statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 580.A11, the representation letter is obtained from current management, who is responsible for the financial statements covering the full period. The current CFO can provide representations over the full year based on review and inquiry. The auditor should also perform additional procedures to address increased risk for the pre-October period.
Wrong (A): The former officer''s refusal does not automatically require a disclaimer because current management can provide representations for the full period.
Wrong (C): Limiting the audit scope to a partial year is not necessary when current management can represent the full period and additional procedures can address risks.
Wrong (D): A former officer''s refusal to provide representations is not a subsequent event; it is an audit matter addressed through current management representations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8669;

-- Q8813: Written Representations [hard/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Automatically issue a disclaimer of opinion because any representation refusal limits scope","Evaluate the refusal''s implications on reliability and determine if additional procedures are needed","Accept the refusal without further action since additional representations are discretionary","Withdraw from the engagement immediately because the refusal indicates management lacks integrity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 580.20-.21, the auditor must evaluate the implications when management does not provide requested representations. For additional (non-required) representations, the auditor should consider the reasons for refusal, evaluate the effect on the reliability of other representations, and determine whether additional procedures or a modified opinion are warranted.
Wrong (A): Automatic disclaimer applies only when management refuses required representations; refusal of additional representations requires professional judgment, not automatic action.
Wrong (C): The auditor cannot simply accept a refusal without further evaluation; all refusals require consideration of their implications.
Wrong (D): Refusal of additional representations does not automatically indicate lack of integrity or require withdrawal from the engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 8813;

-- Q1: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct (ET 1.210) and SEC independence rules, a direct financial interest held by an auditor''s immediate family member, such as a spouse, impairs independence regardless of materiality. Any material direct financial interest by a covered member or immediate family is a prohibited relationship.
Wrong (B): Tax return preparation is a permissible nonattest service when the client makes all tax decisions and the auditor does not assume a management role.
Wrong (C): Management advisory services do not automatically impair independence if the auditor does not assume management responsibilities and the client makes all final decisions.
Wrong (D): Sharing an office building with a client has no bearing on independence under professional standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1;

-- Q2: Ethics and Independence [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct ET 1.295 (Management Participation), a member impairs independence by assuming management responsibilities for an attest client. Making management decisions—such as authorizing transactions, hiring, or directing operations—constitutes assuming a role that belongs exclusively to the client.
Wrong (A): Tax return preparation is generally permissible under ET 1.296 when the client makes all final decisions on tax positions and the auditor does not take a management role.
Wrong (B): Assisting with software implementation does not impair independence provided management accepts responsibility for system design and operation decisions.
Wrong (C): Advising on internal controls is permissible when the client evaluates recommendations and decides whether to implement them.$EXPL$
